<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tēlpochcalli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Telpochcalli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tēlpochcalli (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, 'house of the young men') were schools in each district or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calpulli">calpulli</a> of the Aztec world, where youths were educated from the age of 15 to serve their community and to fight. They were attended by commoners, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macehualtin">macehualtin</a>, and a few noble boys, while the sons of nobles went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calmecac">calmecac</a>. Life there was hard: the day began with a cold bath and a frugal meal, and students memorised songs of praise to the gods, trained with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a> and the macuahuitl, carried materials to repair the temples and worked the fields together for their keep.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Noble boys trained for the priesthood at the calmecac, while commoners could enter it from the <b>telpochcalli</b>, the school of their calpolli.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li></ul></section>
